WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.910
SPEAKER: Business is
booming at Kitty Catwalks.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.070
And meow, with more
customers and employees,

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.810
they've got their paws on a
whole lot more sensitive data.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.810
They know that
Google Cloud DLP will

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.460
help them protect that
data and reduce their risk.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.530
But with so many
transformation options,

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.060
it could be a real challenge
figuring out which ones to use

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:17.700
and when.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.617
Today, we'll talk about
what de-identification

00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:22.050
is, the factors to
consider when choosing

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.420
a de-identification
technique, and check out

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.382
a few examples, including
crypto-based tokenization.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.950
De-identification is
the process of removing

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.950
personal identifying
information from your data.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.950
Common examples of personal
identifying information,

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.160
or PII, are credit card numbers,
addresses, phone numbers,

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.130
and names.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.140
Of course, some
parts of your system

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.170
might need access
to this information,

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.530
billing for example.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.020
But other parts do not.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.490
This is where de-identification
with DLP comes in.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:03.040
The DLP API can intelligently
detect sensitive information

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.900
and use a number of
de-identification

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.090
transformation
techniques to mask,

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.960
delete, tokenize, or
otherwise obscure the data.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.460
Choosing the right
de-identification

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.880
transformation will depend
largely on two things.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.270
First of all, we're going to
need to consider what kind

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.940
of data we want to de-identify.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.490
Are we going to try
to de-identify dates?

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.770
By using date shifting,
we can shift date values

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.230
by a random amount of time.

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.600
Or maybe we're trying to
hide text from images.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.180
Well, image redaction
will work there.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:37.780
DLP uses info type detectors and
optical character recognition

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.120
to find text in image
files and mask it

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.030
with an opaque rectangle.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.010
The second thing to
consider is the purpose

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.530
for de-identifying data.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.050
This is important, because some
de-identification techniques

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.145
will completely hide
sensitive values,

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:53.520
while others will
still allow you

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.320
to extrapolate important
information about the data.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.270
Let's consider an example.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.890
Check out this excerpt
from a chat log

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:04.180
between a customer and a Kitty
Catwalks customer service rep.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.070
When we de-identify
it using masking,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:07.900
the sensitive
content is replaced

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.125
by a masking character, like
an asterisk or a hash symbol.

00:02:11.125 --> 00:02:13.500
There isn't much that we can
deduce about the information

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.720
that's being removed.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.810
When it's de-identified
using replacement,

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.602
where it can be replaced
by something else,

00:02:19.602 --> 00:02:21.240
like its corresponding
info type,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.400
we can at least know that
what the customer entered

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.230
was an email address.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:28.390
In both cases, we
successfully removed

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.770
the personal information.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.480
But with replacement,
we can at least

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.150
tell what info type was removed.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.670
Other techniques use bucketing
to help you generalize

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.200
distinguishing
values in your data,

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.080
based on custom
values or ranges.

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.610
This is especially
useful for data

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:46.048
meant to be used for reports
or for data analysis.

00:02:46.048 --> 00:02:47.590
We'll be able to
make use of the data

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.960
without exposing
it unnecessarily.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.790
For example, when the
finance department

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.860
wants to write a
report on salary info,

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.317
considering there's only
one director of Cat Naps,

00:02:56.317 --> 00:02:58.150
it'd be easy for anyone
looking at this data

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.970
to learn what that
person's salary is.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.830
Potentially sensitive
information.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.407
By applying
bucketing here, we're

00:03:05.407 --> 00:03:07.990
able to use the financial data
while protecting the employee's

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:08.860
privacy.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.170
Another powerful
de-identification category

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.620
worth noting is
crypto-based tokenization.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.290
Cloud DLP can securely
encrypt sensitive data

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.540
using a cryptographic
key and replace it

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.130
with a hash or a token.

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.810
Cloud DLP supports several
types of tokenization,

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.580
including some that
are reversible,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.450
so that you can even re-identify
the de-identified data.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.630
Here's one more example.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.130
We scanned some log files of
Kitty Catwalks' issue ticketing

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:38.700
system and exported the
results to BigQuery.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.210
You'll see that,
in the data column,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.877
it shows text that
we've had replacement

00:03:41.877 --> 00:03:44.580
applied on various info types.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.530
But check out the first column.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:50.550
Here, we apply tokenization
and [INAUDIBLE] the user ID.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.140
The values in this column
are unrecognizable.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.340
But if we run a
query like this one

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.750
to give us account
based on the user ID,

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.953
we can see how many issues
there are for each unique user.

00:04:00.953 --> 00:04:03.370
This is accomplished without
exposing the value that we've

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:06.140
replaced, so it can be
used for data analysis

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:09.780
without risk of
compromising user data.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.610
Furthermore, the
user ID values can

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.035
be re-identified by
individuals or teams that

00:04:14.035 --> 00:04:16.700
are authorized to access the
encryption key used to tokenize

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.029
the values in the first place.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.329
So which de-identification
technique

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.130
is the best choice
for Kitty Catwalks

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.890
to protect their sensitive
customer and employee data?

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:28.770
The good news is
cloud DLP offers

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.688
enough de-identification
options and flexibility

00:04:31.688 --> 00:04:33.480
that they won't have
to prescribe a blanket

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.490
policy for all their PII.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.920
Understanding the
trade-offs of each technique

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.560
will empower them to get the
most value from their data,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.820
while protecting it
every step of the way.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.370
Next time, we'll
take a closer look

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.530
and examine how some of
these de-identification

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.400
transformations work to protect
and secure your sensitive data.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.870
See you then.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

